course: dictionary
lesson: 7468
title: One word, two meanings: pore
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pore is a tiny hole. «Sharks have tiny pores on their snouts» tells you the holes are very small and sit on the snout, the nose part of the face. Pores let things pass through: sweat leaves your skin, and water seeps into soil.
explain: The same word can have a second meaning. «She would pore over an old book, looking for a clue» means she reads or studies it very carefully for a long time. The clues are the old book, looking for a clue, and the idea of a long time. Pore over is always followed by something you study.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits. Try the tiny hole meaning in the sentence: "She would tiny-hole over an old book" makes no sense, so this must be the study meaning. Swap your meaning in and check.
fact: A pore is a tiny hole in skin, a leaf or a rock.
fact: Pores let sweat, air or water pass through.
fact: «Fish have a row of pores along each side» means small holes in a line along the body.
fact: To pore over something means to read or study it very carefully for a long time.
fact: Pore is also a verb: pore, pores, pored, poring.
exercise: «Sharks have tiny pores on their snouts.» What is a pore? | A large fin | A sharp tooth | A kind of fish | *A tiny hole
exercise: «The detective would pore over the old map for hours.» What does pore over mean? | Look at for one second | Tear up quickly | Lose on purpose | *Read or study very carefully for a long time
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Fish have a lateral line, a row of pores along each side.» | row | *pores | lateral | side
test: «The shell has thousands of tiny pores.» Pores here means... | Hard, shiny coverings for eggs | Long, thin pieces of paper | *Tiny holes that let water or air pass through | Very loud noises
test: Which word fits? «Maya will ___ over her science notes before the test.» | Poor | *pore | Pier | Pour
exam: Which meaning of pore fits best in «Lily would pore over the photos of sea creatures for an hour»? | To pour out a drink | A tiny hole in skin | *To study something very carefully for a long time | A hole in a rock that holds water
